WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:14.400
Hi, welcome back to the online Wheel of Life retreat.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.200
I'd like to thank Alison for coming and translating.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:29.200
We're on session 16, which is called "A Life to Live."

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:40.260
Now you see this group of people just walking down the street and

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:46.020
each one is walking through the Wheel of Life.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:56.420
And pretty much in only two of the links,  we cover a whole human life.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.900
Those two links... just before them  comes link number ten,

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.720
and you can see it here--

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.240
this is my kid painters from Nepal--

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.440
and they have painted...  this lady is supposed to be a pregnant lady,

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:32.580
and she represents link number ten, chupa sipa (BCU PA SRID PA).

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.120
chupa (BCU PA) means "number ten."

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:42.720
sipa (SRID PA) is a difficult word.  It has been mistranslated many times.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:54.740
It can mean "possible," it can mean "becoming,"

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:04.660
it can mean "your whole life."  And it doesn't mean any of those here.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:11.120
I think it was a big breakthrough  when my own teacher taught me,

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:22.340
he said in this case you have to think that the word  sipa means "karma which is ripe."

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.660
Back in link number two,

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.740
we had karma which was like a seed  that you just put under the ground.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:45.040
And we translated that as "fresh karma."  nyipa duje kyi le (GNYIS PA 'DU BYED KYI LAS) is fresh karma.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:57.840
And remember, we put the seed  under the ground in the spring,

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.560
watermelon seed,

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:07.500
and then it rained and the water touched the seed,

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:14.640
and it woke up all the  workers in the factory inside the seed.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:28.580
I pictured them--they are like [yawns], they are waking up, and then the supervisor wakes up.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.520
He's like, "Wake up everybody--we got work to do,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.260
we've got to get ready to push this sprout out of this seed."

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:49.580
And so during those few months' time  after the water touches the seed,

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:54.660
strange things are happening inside of the seed.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:03.940
We finished those two "preparing the factory to open."

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:13.500
They were called  gyepa sepa gupa lenpa (BRGYAD PA SRED PA DGU PA LEN PA)--  link number eight and link number nine.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:25.140
We took the good seeds and did coffee meditation.  "I'm happy, I helped someone."

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:39.560
And on top of that we did a high-powered understanding about seeds, which makes good seeds  open faster and bigger.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:53.940
With negative seeds in the picture of the Wheel of Life, link number eight and nine,

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.820
we did sort of a negative coffee meditation.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:07.900
We were like, "Oh, I'm glad I'm eating meat, I will get stronger."

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:22.160
And then even more terrible, we think there's no such thing as karma, the pen comes from the pen.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:31.840
"Even if I kill an animal to eat it,  I don't believe anything will happen to me."

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:43.780
So because of those two ideas, those bad seeds in The Wheel of Life get stronger and stronger,

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:51.700
and at link number ten...  let's go to the book, let's go to the text.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:59.880
It says chupa sipa ni (BCU PA SRID PA NI)-- "What is link number ten?" which is called "ripe karma."

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:09.940
ngar duje kyi namshe la le kyi bakchak gu (SNGAR 'DU BYED KYIS RNAM SHES LA LAS KYI BAG CHAGS BSGOS)--  earlier in the wheel,

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:16.760
the second link, which is called  duje ('DU BYED) in Tibetan, and sanskara (saṃskāra संस्कार) in Sanskrit.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:30.780
Remember, link number one was when you walk into the kitchen and your husband yells at you

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:39.460
and you say, "I didn't do anything,  I don't know why he is yelling at me."

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:46.460
And that's link number one,  which is misunderstanding where he's coming from.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:53.760
That misunderstanding pushes link number two

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:59.060
and she makes karma.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:05.740
How? She says,  "I'm not stupid, you're stupid" to her husband.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:16.480
So the du je in the text here, the sanskara, is the moment that she says "I'm not stupid, you're stupid."

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:32.360
And then the bakchak (BAG CHAGS), which means  "the seed of the karma," is pressed on the mind.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:42.300
Okay, my dog is not only blind and deaf,

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:51.060
now sometimes he poopoos in the house by accident.

00:07:51.860 --> 00:08:02.540
Sometimes on the carpet. And you know, we try to lift the poopoo off very carefully with the toilet paper,

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.560
but still a little bit of smell will rub into the carpet.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:20.160
So the verb here gupa (BSGOS PA) means  that smell gets "rubbed" into the carpet.

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:33.540
In this case, the karma of saying  "I'm not stupid, you're stupid" gets rubbed into our mind,

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.000
and this is going to be important later.

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:45.500
We've been talking about the third link,  which is one of the monkeys

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:54.140
and that monkey represents your mind  entering your mother's womb.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:05.760
But it can also represent the carpet,  where the stinky has rubbed into the carpet.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:14.100
So at the moment that the wife says,  "I'm not stupid, you're stupid,"

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:21.580
that karma is rubbed into the mindstream,

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:27.520
and that's another meaning of the third link.

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:35.460
So the third link, first we understand it as the mind coming into my mother's womb.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:48.840
But we can also understand the third link  as the mind in which the seed is pressed when she said  "I'm not stupid, you're stupid."

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:03.080
Then at that moment when the seed is pressed into the mind, "I'm not stupid, you're stupid,"

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:14.240
it's like the watermelon seed was just put under the dirt.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:23.280
Then, what's the water? What's the fertilizer? We finished it in the last class.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:31.960
In the last class, we finished  what's the water and what's the fertilizer.

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:39.700
And remember, with a bad seed it's two different things,

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:48.920
like she told her friend, "My husband yelled at me,  but I yelled back at him. Ha ha ha."

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:57.700
And her friend says,  "Aren't you sorry you yelled at your husband?"

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:07.960
"No, no, I'm happy, he deserved it."  It's like she's doing a negative coffee meditation.

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:21.380
And she has no idea that her kids are going to be bad for many weeks because of what she said to her husband.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:29.160
So that bad seed, si len gyi su tab (SRID LEN GYIS GSOS BTAB)--

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:42.800
the bad seed now has been given water and fertilizer  by her negative coffee meditation  and by her misunderstanding.

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:54.920
And now that seed from link number two, which is  "I'm not stupid, you're stupid,"

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:01.040
It becomes tu chen du song (MTHU CAN DU SONG).

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:10.740
The seed becomes tu (MTHU).   tu means "tremendous power."

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:16.060
Like nuclear power.

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:24.160
Then what power does it have now  after her negative coffee meditation?

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:38.720
chi may lu pen (PHYI MA'I LUS 'PHEN)-- now that seed can shoot a new lifetime,  it can project a new lifetime.

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:46.360
And that's the meaning of chupa sipa, link number ten.

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:59.860
It's represented by a pregnant woman, but that woman's big belly represents a big seed ready to open.

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:16.880
Then the seed splits open, the spout comes out,  and we see the picture here of a child being born.

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:24.780
I think a lot about these images, like an arrow in the eye

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:32.620
or a baby coming out of a woman.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:44.740
In these ancient paintings,  these must have been very very shocking.

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:56.660
They didn't have videos,  but I think if Buddha had a computer and video

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:06.440
he would have made a movie  about this Wheel of Life and not not paintings.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:18.780
And I think some day it would be interesting to make a real evocative movie, like a big emotional movie

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:29.580
where somebody has the arrow in their eye...

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:39.000
I fact, online I did try to find someone who got shot with the arrow. It did happen once and the person  went to the hospital.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:58.200
But these pictures are supposed  to freak us out, you know, like, "AH!"

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:05.940
When you see this picture of a baby coming out of a woman, and the woman's body is being torn apart,

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:18.640
then we're supposed to think,  "I don't want to do that again."

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:27.100
What don't you want to do again? You don't want to be the woman or you don't want to be the baby?

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:43.600
I don't want to be either one. Okay?   chu chikpa kyewa (BCU GCIG PA SKYE BA) means  "now the seed is cracking open," the fat seed,

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:47.540
and now a child is coming out.

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:59.480
The text says detar su tabpe (DE LTAR GSOS BTAB PAS)-- now that the seed was given its water and its fertilizer  by the negative coffee meditation,

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:11.840
yang si druppay le nu pa tu chen du sonwa de la ten ne  (YANG SRID 'GRUB PA'I LAS NUS PA MTHU CAN DU SONG BA DE LA BRTEN NAS)-- based on that karma now being totally ripe,

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:18.440
kye ne shi-po gang rung du dang po namshe nying tsam jar (SKYE GNAS BZHI PO GANG RUNG DU DANG POR RNAM SHES NYING MTSAMS SBYAR)--

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:29.420
then the mind makes the crossing  into one of the four kinds of birth.

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:42.820
And the moment after the crossing  we call rebirth, link number eleven.

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:49.280
You could say birth. You could say rebirth.

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:55.980
"Geshe-la, the first time it happened,  it wasn't a rebirth, right?"

00:16:57.580 --> 00:17:06.360
There is no first time.  We have been here countless times,  there is no beginning.

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:13.560
The first time I heard that  it was very uncomfortable for me.

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:18.900
We like to think that there was a big bang

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:25.020
or God made the world in six days or something.

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:33.040
It makes us feel comfortable  if we think there was a beginning.

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:38.800
But it's not logical.

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:45.720
Nothing can start unless something else  moves before that.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:56.680
So you could really translate this link number eleven, you could always translate it as "rebirth."

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:05.360
Now the text says "into one of the four kinds of birth."

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:14.520
And this is a popular topic  in the ancient Abhidharma texts.

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:24.540
And because we don't know the four kinds of birth,

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:30.240
and he doesn't mention them in the text,

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:38.220
I thought we should go to another book  and get a nice commentary on those four.

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:45.920
So we're going to follow  the Tibetan here to the commentary.

00:18:49.660 --> 00:18:59.320
chir kye ne la ye na (SPYIR SKYE GNAS LA DBYE NA)-- you can divide all the kinds of birth into four.

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:08.060
And I remember this was the first book I ever studied with my teacher.

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:16.780
We started 45 years ago,  and it took us 12 years to finish this book.

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:24.640
So kye ne (SKYE GNAS) was always a confusing word for me.

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:30.940
kye (SKYE) means "born." ne (GNAS) means "location."

00:19:31.520 --> 00:19:41.320
After many years he learned to translate it more correctly.   It's not "location" of birth; it's the "type" of birth.

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:49.200
So here's the name of the four:

00:19:50.120 --> 00:19:54.420
ngel ske, gong kye, dzu kye, tru sher kye dang shi (MNGAL SKYES, SGONG SKYES, RDZUS SKYES, DROD BSHER SKYES DANG BZHI).

00:19:54.580 --> 00:19:57.880
The first one is called ngel kye (MNGAL SKYES).

00:19:58.460 --> 00:20:04.920
ngel (MNGAL) means "a womb,"  like a human mother's womb.

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:12.980
kye (SKYES) is the past tense of born so "born in the past" in the womb, born in the womb.

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:24.880
Then gong kye (SGONG SKYES)... gong (SGONG), which is sometimes split into go nga (SGO NGA), means "egg."

00:20:28.220 --> 00:20:33.460
So some beings are born from eggs,  some beings are born from wombs.

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:47.740
By the way, in Buddhism the real birth  is at the moment of conception.

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:54.700
Then there's a second birth  out of the womb or out of the egg.

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:03.960
But it's the conception which is when the baby starts.

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:11.800
There's a living sentient being  at the moment of conception.

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:17.680
The third one is called dzu kye (RDZUS SKYES).

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:26.720
Sometimes it's called dzu te kyepa (RDZUS TE SKYES PA).  dzu te kye pa. And it means "miraculous birth."

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:36.660
For example, the Christians believe that Jesus was born without a father... without sex.

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:59.160
And Hindus believed that the great yoga master Patanjali was born in the holy water,  he appeared in the holy water that his mother offered.

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:06.700
That water offering with your hands is called anjali.

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:11.800
And pat means "fell down."

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:22.100
So his name, his whole life he was called "the guy who fell down from the sky into the water." Patanjali.

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:29.500
Then those people are born complete.

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:34.600
Then dru sher le kyewa (DROD SHER LA SKYES BA)

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:40.340
That means, in the summertime

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:47.540
when the weather is warm and there's  lots of rain puddles on the ground,

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:58.740
the ancient people,  they couldn't see the eggs of the insects,

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:07.220
so they called it "a birth from warmth and moisture."

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:29.680
So there's the four kinds of birth: birth from a womb; birth from an egg; miracle birth, complete; and birth from warmth and moisture.

00:23:30.760 --> 00:23:38.020
Now just for fun, I thought you guys would like to continue reading this text,

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:48.400
which is related to the text being translated by our translator Sugeng Shi from Singapore.

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:59.100
Same author from about five hundred years ago.

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:07.420
Okay, so I thought you'd like to hear  some discussion about these four.

00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:13.080
yidak la ngel kye kyang yu de (YI DVAGS LA MNGAL SKYES KYANG YOD DE)--

00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:23.300
hungry ghosts also have birth from a womb, it's possible. They are not just born from miracles--   usually we think they are just born suddenly.

00:24:23.320 --> 00:24:37.100
He says "they can also be born from a womb." So "also" implies that usually they are born another way,  but they also are born from a womb.

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:55.060
yidakmoy tok ju le (YI DVAGS MO'I RTOGS BRJOD LAS)--there's an avadana (avadāna)... these are called "stories of spiritual people"

00:24:58.500 --> 00:25:06.700
from ancient India, and this one is called  the story of "The Lady Hungry Ghost."

00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:14.840
And there's a famous verse that she used to recite.

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:19.848
dak gi nyinpar bu nga gya, tsen mo yang nin nga gya te, su kyang dak la tsimpa me

00:25:19.848 --> 00:25:24.020
(BDAG GIS NYIN PAR BU LNGA BRGYA, MTSAN MO YANG NI LNGA BRGYA STE,  ZOS KYANG BDAG LA TSIM PA MED)

00:25:24.080 --> 00:25:32.460
I give birth from a womb to five hundred kids every day.

00:25:35.300 --> 00:25:43.040
Five hundred hungry ghost kids  come out of my womb every day, in the daylight.

00:25:47.180 --> 00:25:52.140
tsen mo yang ni nga gya te (MTSAN MO YANG NI LNGA BRGYA STE)-- And at night, in the dark, I have another five hundred.

00:25:53.840 --> 00:25:56.940
From her womb.

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:10.100
You might think, "How does hungry ghost mom take care of her one thousand per day hungry ghost kids?"

00:26:16.100 --> 00:26:19.600
su kyang (ZOS KYANG)--I eat them.

00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:26.200
dak la tsimpa me (BDAG LA TSIM PA MED)-- but still I don't feel full.

00:26:27.100 --> 00:26:29.029
che sungpay chir (CES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR)

00:26:29.029 --> 00:26:32.260
mi la ni kye ne shi ga yu de (MI LA NI SKYES GNAS BZHI GA YOD DE)--

00:26:32.260 --> 00:26:38.040
all four kinds of birth are found with human beings.

00:26:38.380 --> 00:26:41.600
They have all four kinds.

00:26:43.900 --> 00:26:50.080
And this is, by the way, a very standard  and famous Abhidharma discussion.

00:26:54.980 --> 00:27:01.100
mi pel cher ngel ne kye (MI PHAL CHER MNGAL NAS SKYE)-- most humans, as we know, are born from the womb.

00:27:03.560 --> 00:27:08.500
dzu kye--some are born miraculously.

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:15.720
I used to translate that as "born complete" because they're just born as a complete person.

00:27:19.060 --> 00:27:23.780
And they give the example  of the Buddhist master named Aryadeva.

00:27:24.640 --> 00:27:27.960
Famous disciple of Nagarjuna.

00:27:33.400 --> 00:27:40.740
I don't know the story, we can look it up some day,  but apparently he was born miraculously.

00:27:45.440 --> 00:27:49.000
gyelpo chiwo kye tabu dru sher le kye (RGYAL PO SPYI BO SKYES LTA BU DROD GSHER LAS SKYES)--

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:58.020
Then there was a king who was born from the top of the head of his father from warmth and moisture.

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:07.080
Then there were two Arhats,  or two people who became Arhats.

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:18.280
In Tibetan, they're called   Drak (BRAG) and Nyeway Drak (NYE BA'I BRAG),  and they were born from eggs.

00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:27.480
dundro la yang shi ga yu (DUD 'GRO LA YANG BZHI GA YOD)-- animals also have four kinds of birth, all four.

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:34.200
nyelwa dang hla dang bardo la dzu kye kyi kyab te (DMYAL BA DANG LHA DANG BAR DO LA RDZUS SKYES KYIS KHYAB STE)

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:41.900
But all hell beings, all devas,  and all bardo beings are born complete.

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:46.600
Hell beings, devas, and bardo beings,

00:28:47.700 --> 00:28:53.720
they are always born miraculously.

00:28:58.220 --> 00:29:02.220
Hell being, deva, and bardo.

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:12.800
So bardo (BAR DO) being... remember it is that one in the moment after you die and you go into the bardo.

00:29:16.660 --> 00:29:21.220
For seven days. Up to 49 days.

00:29:22.400 --> 00:29:29.420
Then he quotes the Abhidharma Kosha, which is the most famous book of Abhidharma nowadays.

00:29:34.880 --> 00:29:37.760
It's about two thousand years old.

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:41.860
nyelwa nam dang hla nam dang,  sipa parma dzu te kye (DMYAL BA RNAMS DANG LHA RNAMS DANG,  SRID PA BAR MA RDZUS TE SKYE)--

00:29:41.860 --> 00:29:51.900
all hell beings, all devas, and all bardo beings  are born complete. Miraculously.

00:29:58.220 --> 00:30:06.700
Which brings us to link number twelve,  the last of the twelve links.

00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:14.340
The picture you see is of somebody  carrying a big package on their back.

00:30:16.740 --> 00:30:30.200
This represents the way that people were buried,   or how they treated dead bodies in  ancient India and also in Tibet.

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:49.640
If you think your job is bad, at least you are not carrying corpses to the mountain or to the charnel ground.

00:30:49.680 --> 00:30:58.180
Charnel ground means a very terrible place where you throw dead bodies; you just throw them on the ground.

00:31:09.580 --> 00:31:12.500
I'll tell you a story.

00:31:12.840 --> 00:31:21.720
When I was a young monk, actually I was living in a monastery, but I wasn't a monk yet--

00:31:24.420 --> 00:31:28.720
small Mongolian monastery in America--

00:31:31.020 --> 00:31:36.000
and the senior teacher of the Dalai Lama,

00:31:39.180 --> 00:31:45.380
he was very, very famous for secret teachings,

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:51.440
and he was very, very serious.

00:31:52.960 --> 00:31:59.020
Then I got the job to be his driver for one day

00:32:02.140 --> 00:32:10.220
and I have to drive him for two or three hours,  and everyone told me "don't talk to him."

00:32:10.860 --> 00:32:19.400
I was very young, I was 26.  Why I remember you will find out.

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:27.760
They said, "Don't talk to him." I said, "Why?"  "He is Yamantaka.

00:32:27.900 --> 00:32:32.760
He's the fierce Buddha! Arrarrrghhhh!

00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:36.740
Don't talk to him!"

00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:43.280
Then two famous teachers got in the car,

00:32:44.220 --> 00:32:52.760
but they got in the back. Why... why doesn't the big teacher sit in the back?

00:32:53.960 --> 00:32:59.000
And they told me in India  the big person always sits in front.

00:33:02.420 --> 00:33:08.540
So he got in next to me. And I was like [nervous, scared]

00:33:09.560 --> 00:33:21.560
I tried not to talk, then we started to drive, and one of the lamas in the back, he said, "This is Mike."

00:33:22.540 --> 00:33:25.560
Then the teacher goes, "Huh!"

00:33:26.060 --> 00:33:34.460
Then he says "Oh, Mike is very good.  He understands Abhidharma."

00:33:35.920 --> 00:33:40.120
Then the Dalai Lama's teacher is like "Huh!"

00:33:41.960 --> 00:33:49.600
"ngapo nam ni don shi la."  (LNGA PO RNAMS NI DON BZHI LA)  He quoted the first line of the second chapter.

00:33:51.300 --> 00:33:58.920
I was memorizing the Abhidharma Kosha at that time.  I never finished, but I finished three or four chapters.

00:34:02.820 --> 00:34:07.340
I just finished memorizing that line.

00:34:07.700 --> 00:34:15.880
Then I thought "Oh, that's the first line of the second chapter" and explained it, and he was like "hmmm."

00:34:18.280 --> 00:34:25.120
Then we drove for some time  and the two big lamas in the back fell asleep.

00:34:26.360 --> 00:34:31.420
And the Dalai Lama's teacher started to talk to me,

00:34:32.200 --> 00:34:35.960
and we had a wonderful talk for like two hours.

00:34:36.740 --> 00:34:42.000
And he said "Tell me everything we see in America,  tell me what it is."

00:34:44.820 --> 00:34:55.180
And we passed the cemetery with many crosses and he said, "Oh, what's that? Why many crosses?"

00:34:56.000 --> 00:34:59.920
Then I said, "That's a cemetery."

00:35:00.280 --> 00:35:04.360
He says, "Why they have those crosses?"

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:10.720
I said, "Well, they buried the body there."

00:35:13.020 --> 00:35:15.680
"What! The whole body?"

00:35:16.740 --> 00:35:19.860
"Yeah."   "Not the ashes?"

00:35:20.660 --> 00:35:31.080
"No, no, they believe that the body, the person will come alive at the end of the world and they will go to heaven."

00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:41.340
Then he looked at me and said, "Ha, ha, ha!

00:35:42.940 --> 00:35:50.820
What a waste of good living space! People can live on that land! Why did they put the dead body under there?"

00:35:53.640 --> 00:35:58.000
Then I'm like, "Well, what should we do with the dead body?"

00:35:59.240 --> 00:36:02.780
And it's what you see in this picture.

00:36:03.340 --> 00:36:07.280
"We cut all the pieces up,

00:36:10.040 --> 00:36:13.700
We mix it with tsampa flour," with flour,

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:18.200
"and we carry it to the top of the mountain,

00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:25.560
and we give it to the birds and then it's a good karma.

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:34.480
That person's body is not wasted,  that person's body is a gift to to these wild animals."

00:36:38.300 --> 00:36:44.140
So that's what's happening here in this picture.

00:36:44.140 --> 00:36:48.880
de ne chu nyipa ga shi chi rikpay tendrel rim gyi druppa yin (DE NAS BCU GNYIS PA RGA SHI CI RIGS PA'I RTEN 'BREL RIM GYIS 'GRUB PA YIN)--

00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:53.500
then we come to the last link,  which is link number twelve.

00:36:55.140 --> 00:36:59.720
ga shi (RGA SHI). ga shi means "aging and death."

00:37:00.940 --> 00:37:10.560
And technically it's called "death, maybe with aging."

00:37:16.040 --> 00:37:26.460
ga shi chi rikpay tendrel (RGA SHI CI RIGS PA'I RTEN 'BREL) means...  I asked my teacher, "Why do they call it  'death with maybe some aging'?"

00:37:28.640 --> 00:37:34.220
Then my teacher said, "Well sometimes kids die in the womb,"

00:37:36.280 --> 00:37:44.440
so we don't know.  That's why Buddha didn't make thirteen links.

00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:53.660
Some people die in the womb,  they don't have time to get old.

00:37:53.840 --> 00:37:58.980
de yang gawa ni pungpoy ne skab rim gyi shen du gyur shing minpa yin (DE YANG RGA BA NI PHUNG PO'I GNAS SKABS RIM GYIS GZHAN DU 'GYUR ZHING SMIN PA YIN)--

00:37:58.980 --> 00:38:03.600
what is the definition of aging?

00:38:04.580 --> 00:38:09.980
The body goes through change, change, change, change--it never stays the same.

00:38:14.080 --> 00:38:21.100
It falls apart step-by-step, day-by-day.

00:38:22.080 --> 00:38:26.180
Now the definition of death:

00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:30.740
shiwa ni punpoy ri tun dor ba-am jikpa la ser (SHI BA NI PHUNG PO'I RIS MTHUN DOR BA'AM 'JIG PA LA ZER)--

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:39.900
when we say death, what we mean is that  the flow of the same body and mind is interrupted.

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:52.300
So we don't say the body died or the mind stopped.

00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:57.560
It takes on different clothing.

00:38:59.340 --> 00:39:05.880
So it's very interesting that death  is not the end of the body or the mind.

00:39:08.580 --> 00:39:15.200
The mind shifts, the physical form shifts,

00:39:16.720 --> 00:39:21.060
but life does not end and cannot end.

00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:29.480
You have lived for time with no beginning  and you will live for time with no end.

00:39:35.240 --> 00:39:39.560
I'd like to end this session with kind of a review.

00:39:42.160 --> 00:39:45.620
And I'm going to be using special images.

00:39:48.100 --> 00:39:50.740
We've had all twelve links now,

00:39:52.900 --> 00:39:57.620
And I've taught them in groups, on different days.

00:40:00.880 --> 00:40:10.080
And I'd like to just review that because it's going to be important tomorrow and the next days.

00:40:11.980 --> 00:40:23.560
So in this first picture here you see of the twelve links and then... except this morning I learned  how to mark them with a yellow marker,

00:40:26.960 --> 00:40:30.820
so it looks like it was done by a kid,  but it's done by me, okay?

00:40:35.180 --> 00:40:40.700
First, I want to draw your attention that the  first two links hang together.

00:40:43.600 --> 00:40:50.700
Again you can call it ignorance and karma.

00:40:53.020 --> 00:41:00.280
But I'd like to call it misunderstanding  and planting seeds. Fresh seeds.

00:41:02.960 --> 00:41:08.020
And again, I want you to think that  these two kind of hang together.

00:41:10.500 --> 00:41:17.800
You know, the wife comes in the kitchen. She's tired.  She comes home from work.

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:24.500
She says, "Give me a hug..." no she just reaches out like this. She doesn't say anything.

00:41:25.260 --> 00:41:29.160
But she's thinking, "I will get a hug from my husband."

00:41:29.960 --> 00:41:35.160
Then suddenly he says, "You're so stupid,"

00:41:35.820 --> 00:41:41.740
and in that moment, she misunderstands  where that comes from.

00:41:44.420 --> 00:41:48.560
She says, "I didn't do anything and he yelled at me!"

00:41:48.760 --> 00:41:53.420
But she did do something;  she yelled at her kids last week.

00:41:54.400 --> 00:41:59.720
Then misunderstanding triggers the second link.

00:42:01.100 --> 00:42:09.160
Then she says, "I'm not stupid, you're stupid."  So those two links kind of hang together.

00:42:10.160 --> 00:42:16.020
Okay, now the next image,  there's a poorly written yellow cloud...

00:42:21.600 --> 00:42:26.220
around links three up to seven.

00:42:27.120 --> 00:42:37.500
And these are the five links that are  kind of our equipment during life.  They're what we use to express ourselves.

00:42:41.440 --> 00:42:45.780
In the third link we get a mind,

00:42:46.340 --> 00:42:53.180
in the fourth link we start to get a body  along with that mind inside our mum,

00:42:59.320 --> 00:43:09.000
in the fifth link our eyes, ears, nose--we start to get our equipment for contacting the outside world.

00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:18.500
In the sixth link we used those instruments to make contact with the outside world.

00:43:23.920 --> 00:43:28.920
And then in the seventh link, we start to have feelings about what we contacted.

00:43:34.280 --> 00:43:44.880
So you can kind of get a feeling that link three to link seven kind of hang together,  they are the equipment that we need to be alive.

00:43:51.020 --> 00:44:01.200
In the next image, I drew a yellow cloud  around links eight, nine, and ten.

00:44:04.660 --> 00:44:15.440
And really when you think about it, the most important version of eight, nine, and ten is at the end of our life.

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:25.240
We have seeds in our mind from our whole life including, "I'm not stupid, you're stupid."

00:44:30.600 --> 00:44:41.780
Then we talked about how,  it's very important which seed is activated  in the last moment of our conscious life.

00:44:47.920 --> 00:44:54.900
So as you see in the image here,  link eight and link nine and link ten  kind of hang together.

00:44:57.860 --> 00:45:05.900
Ten is that big fat seed that already got a lot  of water and fertilizer, but didn't open yet.

00:45:10.080 --> 00:45:14.900
And then eight and nine are like  the water and fertilizer on that seed.

00:45:18.280 --> 00:45:24.940
Then if you think about it, link number eleven and link number twelve--

00:45:27.620 --> 00:45:35.640
rebirth, aging day-by-day  after you are conceived, and then death--

00:45:37.780 --> 00:45:45.880
that's a whole lifetime.  Just eleven and twelve is a whole lifetime.

00:45:48.080 --> 00:45:52.440
Eleven is coming into the womb.

00:45:54.680 --> 00:45:58.900
But we already came into the womb in number three.

00:46:01.140 --> 00:46:05.660
So I want you to start to get this feeling

00:46:07.060 --> 00:46:13.720
that this is not one life from beginning to end  like Shakespeare's poem.

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:26.840
Something else is going on here.   Eleven and twelve are a whole life themselves.

00:46:30.180 --> 00:46:37.160
One and two kind of sound like they happen  before your mind came into this womb.

00:46:43.580 --> 00:46:52.660
So now the question comes, "How many lifetimes are in one turning of the twelve links?"

00:46:56.220 --> 00:46:59.840
And why it's not just one lifetime like Shakespeare.

00:47:02.540 --> 00:47:11.760
To find out the answer you have to come back to tomorrow's session, number 17!

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:16.080
See you then.

